      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "Dream.Org demonstrates a high level of operational transparency, evidenced by its <cite id=\"8\">96.46 score in Accountability & Finance</cite> from Charity Navigator. This is supported by a <cite id=\"8\">98.20 Leadership & Adaptability score</cite>, reflecting a well-structured board of five members and clear strategic goals. Financially, the charity is resilient with <cite id=\"8\">4.8 months of working capital</cite> in reserves, allowing it to sustain operations despite recent revenue fluctuations. Their impact is further validated by <cite id=\"7\">tracking 19 distinct outcome metrics</cite> through Candid, showing a commitment to data-driven advocacy."
      ],
      "summary": "Dream.Org is a national advocacy and capacity-building organization focused on bridging the gap between underrepresented communities and the clean energy economy. The organization operates through specialized initiatives like <cite id=\"1\">Green For All and Tech For All</cite>, which aim to ensure that environmental and technological advancements provide equitable economic opportunities. In FY2024, the organization reported <cite id=\"9\">total revenue of $5,715,452</cite> to support its mission of justice reform and climate equity. While the organization maintains a <cite id=\"8\">94/100 overall score from Charity Navigator</cite>, its financial profile shows significant volatility. For instance, revenue dropped from <cite id=\"9\">$53.4 million in 2022</cite> to <cite id=\"9\">$4.09 million in FY2024</cite>. Despite this shift, the charity maintains a <cite id=\"8\">program expense ratio of 85.1%</cite>, ensuring that the majority of its spending is directed toward its core advocacy and community programs. The organization is particularly active in federal policy, highlighting that <cite id=\"3\">$369 billion in federal funding</cite> has been authorized for climate investments that their toolkits help communities navigate. As a <cite id=\"5\">sadaqah-eligible organization</cite>, it offers a pathway for donors to support systemic change in the United States, though it lacks the direct humanitarian service model common in traditional faith-based charities.",
